WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.320
Scientists can use DNA to pinpoint criminal suspects,

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.740
trace migration patterns, modify organisms to produce medicine,

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.420
the list goes on and on.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.520
But to do any of this stuff with DNA,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.740
we need a lot of it.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.550
That's where PCR comes in.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.170
PCR stands for polymerase chain reaction,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.950
and it's exactly what it sounds like:

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.100
a chain reaction that creates a polymer,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.620
a long molecule made up of repeating units.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.620
Those units are four different nucleotide bases, referred to as A,

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.030
T,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.090
C and G.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.610
DNA is double stranded,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.450
which means it's not just one polymer,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.220
but two,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:33.980
and these strands are complementary;

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.350
they complete each other. Specifically,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.040
A only connects to T and C only connects to G, forming the DNA sequence.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.350
So how does PCR give us more DNA?

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.410
PCR is a technique that mimics the way cells in nature make copies of their DNA by pulling 

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.290
apart the complimentary strands and using each is a template.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.080
To do this,

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.980
they use a molecular machine called DNA polymerase,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.860
which zips along a strand of DNA and pulls in complimentary bases as it goes. When we want 

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.020
more DNA in the lab, instead of a cell,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.930
we have a tube. Like a cell,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.690
the tube's got template DNA,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.990
DNA polymerase, and assorted nucleotides,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.190
plus one more special ingredient called a primer,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.410
which tells the DNA polymerase where to start.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.880
Depending on what piece of DNA we want to copy,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.000
we design the sequence of As, Ts,

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.280
Cs and Gs in the primer to specifically match the beginning of our sequence.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.510
Now we apply a near boiling heat and see what happens. Near 

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:28.180
boiling?

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.740
But molecular machines usually break at that temperature.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:35.830
This secret to PCR's efficiency is actually a special polymerase that was discovered in bacteria living 

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.420
in thermal vents at Yellowstone National Park.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.320
But anyway,

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:43.750
the complimentary strands are pulled apart and primers stick based on the order of their nucleotides.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:49.110
They act as sign posts for DNA polymerase to start running along the DNA template strands, pulling in 

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.360
complementary bases one by one by one,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.490
until two new copies of the piece of DNA are finished.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.440
But we've only made one copy, when we actually want way more than that.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.778
So we repeat the process with the new DNA as the template, setting off the chain reaction however 

00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:04.918
many cycles we want,

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:08.498
giving us two to the nth power copies of the DNA fragment.

00:02:08.618 --> 00:02:09.848
In as little as an hour,

00:02:09.858 --> 00:02:14.118
we'll have more than enough to get a suspect's DNA fingerprint or develop a totally new 

00:02:14.118 --> 00:02:14.918
therapeutic.

